#: ../../../ros2_interop.md:93
msgid "Maintaining a single robot description format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ros2_interop.md:95
msgid ""
"The main pain point of using existing simulation assets with ROS 2 tools was "
"updating URDF files into a Gazebo-readable format. This is no longer "
"required. If you are maintaining a URDF and an SDF file in a project, you "
"can now drop the URDF and just use the SDF for both ROS and Gazebo."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ros2_interop.md:97
msgid ""
"This is made possible by `sdformat_urdf`, a parser plugin library that "
"converts an SDF file to URDF C++ DOM structures, making it understandle by "
"the ROS 2 ecosystem. Although, there are some limitations of the plugin, "
"like not all SDFormat tags are compatible. For example, if you have any "
"sensors attached to a joint, it won't be parsed. More details [here](https://"
"github.com/ros/sdformat_urdf/tree/rolling/sdformat_urdf)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ros2_interop.md:100
msgid ""
"To embed this functionality, we simply need to print the SDFormat file to "
"the `/robot_description` ROS topic, and internally ROS will find a suitable "
"parser, `sdformat_urdf` in this case, to read the file. This is already done "
"while configuring the `robot_state_publisher` earlier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ros2_interop.md:102
msgid "Run RViz and Gazebo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ros2_interop.md:104
msgid ""
"The second aim defined in [Setup](#setup) is essentially maintaining only "
"one robot description format which now can be controlled directly with "
"Gazebo. Using the SDFormat XML description of a robot, the simulator "
"publishes model joint states. And `robot_state_publisher` handles turning "
"those joint states into `tf`s which is used by RViz and other ROS tools. "
"This enables visualizing a model in RViz simulated by Gazebo."
msgstr ""

